```c
suites = kmalloc(sizeof(u32) * n_suites, GFP_KERNEL);
if (WARN_ON(cs[r].pn_len > IEEE80211_MAX_PN_LEN))
```
Reason:
The memory for `suites` is allocated using `kmalloc`, but if an invalid cipher scheme is registered (where `pn_len` exceeds the maximum allowed length), the code does not free the allocated memory before returning an error. This results in a memory leak since the allocated memory is not released.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (WARN_ON(cs[r].pn_len > IEEE80211_MAX_PN_LEN)) {
    kfree(suites);
    return -EINVAL;
}
```
Reason:
The patch adds a call to `kfree(suites)` before returning an error when an invalid cipher scheme is detected. This ensures that the allocated memory for `suites` is properly freed, preventing the memory leak that would occur if the function exits without freeing it.

